    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 231735\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1035 AM MST Fri Oct 23 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 18Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Much quieter weather is expected today into Saturday with only a\n  slight chance of a few showers over the central Arizona high\n  terrain. \n\n- Below normal temperatures through early next week will warm\n  closer to the seasonal average by the middle of next week as dry\n  conditions prevail across the region.\n \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Saturday/... \nThe strong weather system that brought severe weather to portions\nof the Desert Southwest over the past couple of days is now\nprogressing far enough to the northeast to allow for strong drying\nthrough Arizona. This has led to an end to the shower activity and\nclearing skies for much of the area. A cooler air mass has also\nsettled into the region and with the mostly clear skies tonight,\novernight lows should dip into the 50s for all of the lower\ndeserts except for the urban core of Phoenix. \n\nEven though the main cold front has pushed through the area, the\nupper level trough will remain entrenched across the Southwestern\nU.S. through Saturday. The low center is currently situated over\nNevada with some residual moisture still spinning around the low.\nGuidance shows the main energy within the low still ejecting to\nthe northeast away from our region today, but another shortwave or\ntwo is expected to swing back into our region tonight into\nSaturday from the northwest. This should form another low center \nsomewhere over southern California tonight before turning east \nthrough Arizona on Saturday, but not much is expected to come from\nits passage. The latest CAMs are hinting as some isolated showers\ndeveloping Saturday afternoon over the high terrain north of\nPhoenix, but given the expected overall low moisture environment\n(low level mixing ratios of 5-6 g/kg) rain chances will\nessentially be zero over the lower deserts. Saturday should bring\nsome afternoon mid-level CU for south-central Arizona, but \noverall our weather conditions will remain quiet with afternoon \nhighs in the mid to upper 70s in Phoenix to as warm as the lower \n80s in southeast California. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Sunday through Thursday/... \nThe benign weather conditions will continue to prevail well into\nnext week with the lingering trough finally moving out of the\nregion on Sunday and being replaced by weak ridging to zonal\nwesterly flow. This pattern shift will lead to continued mostly\nclear skies and dry conditions, while allowing temperatures to\ngradually warm back into the normal range by the middle of next\nweek. Expect the bulk of the lower deserts to have daily highs in\nthe low to possibly mid 80s and lows from the mid 50s to mid 60s\nduring the first half of next week. Model uncertainty comes into \nplay later next week as there are hints at some moisture return\ninto the region out of the southeast and then a potential \natmospheric river approaching by next weekend. Either or both of \nthese moisture sources may eventually help to bring a return to \nrain chances at some point before the end of the month, but it is \ntoo early to have a good idea on timing and/or placement for any \nweather systems over a week out. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1735Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo weather concerns are expected through the TAF period. Westerly\nto northwesterly winds will persist through the afternoon and\nevening hours with periods of VRB intermixed. Just past mid-night\ntonight winds will shift back out of the east, and look to resume\ntypical diurnal tendencies tomorrow. Wind speeds will be aob 07\nkts. Skies will be mostly clear with FEW mid level cloud decks\ndeveloping later in the period. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo aviation weather concerns are anticipated through the for TAF\nperiod. Winds will favor a westerly component at KIPL while KBLH\nwill remain out of the NW. Speeds should remain light around 5-7\nkts or lower. FEW passing mid level clouds during the overnight\nhours at KIPL, otherwise mostly clear skies at both terminals. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDrier air has spread through the area since yesterday morning\nresulting in clearing skies and an end to the rain chances.\nThe weather system will still linger across the region over the\nnext couple of days bringing light shower chances to mountain \nareas Saturday, but no wetting rainfall is expected. Minimum \nhumidity levels will fall into a 25-50% range following good to \nexcellent overnight recovery. Light winds are forecast for today \nand for much of the area on Saturday with only some breeziness \npossible early Saturday across the western districts. Overall \nquiet weather conditions are forecast Sunday into next week with \nfairly stable humidities and temperatures rebounding back into the\nnormal range by around Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Ryan\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
